Carpal Tunnel Syndrome
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Median nerve paresthesia, Nocturnal exacerbation, Positive Tinel/Phalen signs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-005
Reason for Visit: Intractable nocturnal hand pain and numbness preventing sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, unremitting pain and numbness in the dominant hand leading to severe sleep deprivation and distress. Urgent assessment is required to provide immediate analgesia and confirm the absence of acute ischemic or infective pathology in the extremity before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right hand burns and tingles so badly it wakes me up every hour. I have to shake it just to feel normal."
History of Present Illness: Patient describes numbness, tingling, and paresthesia strictly localized to the palmar aspect of the thumb, index, middle, and radial half of the ring finger. There is a prominent nocturnal exacerbation of paresthesias frequently awakening the patient from sleep. The patient reports consistent relief of painful symptoms achieved by forcefully shaking or wringing the hands, widely recognized as the flick sign. Recently, there has been weakness in grip strength and notable clumsiness, resulting in the frequent dropping of everyday objects. A deep aching pain occasionally radiates proximally from the wrist into the volar forearm.
Functional Status: Independent, but experiencing difficulty with fine motor tasks such as buttoning shirts.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation affecting daytime concentration; dropping hot items in the kitchen.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 72 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Fatigued, rubbing right wrist, no acute systemic distress.
Physical Exam: Physical examination demonstrates a positive Tinel's sign with electrical paresthesia radiating into the median nerve distribution upon tapping the volar wrist. A positive Phalen's maneuver reliably reproduces symptoms during sustained maximal passive wrist flexion for 60 seconds. There is no visible atrophy of the thenar eminence musculature at this stage. Measurable two-point discrimination is greater than 5 millimeters in the specific median nerve sensory distribution. Radial and ulnar pulses are bounding; capillary refill is less than 2 seconds. No erythema or warmth over the wrist joint.
Environmental Observations: Ergonomically poor home-office setup visible in the adjacent room.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cervical Radiculopathy (C6/C7) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal neck range of motion without pain, normal biceps/triceps reflexes, and symptoms strictly localizing distal to the wrist.

De Quervain's Tenosynovitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative Finkelstein test and primary symptoms being neuropathic rather than localized tendon pain.

Peripheral Neuropathy (Diabetic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Asymmetrical unilateral presentation and absence of lower extremity involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe nocturnal neuropathic pain and paresthesia localized to the median nerve distribution. The intense sleep disruption and pain severity prompted the after-hours call. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to prolonged, unmanaged neuropathic pain and severe sleep deprivation, increasing the risk of secondary accidents. Urgent clinical examination confidently ruled out acute vascular compromise or cervical spine pathology, definitively confirming a focal compressive neuropathy. This immediate triage and physical examination satisfied MBS guidelines for urgent intervention, allowing for prompt splinting advice and analgesia to restore patient comfort and function.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food) for acute inflammatory relief. Recommended purchase of a rigid volar wrist splint from a 24-hour pharmacy to be worn strictly at night.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By wearing the wrist splint every night, the wrist is kept straight, instantly reducing nerve compression and guaranteeing unbroken sleep.
Step 2: By taking breaks every 30 minutes during typing to stretch the wrists, the patient reduces repetitive strain, resulting in significantly less daytime aching.
Step 3: By using thicker pens and modifying grip on utensils, the patient reduces force across the carpal tunnel, preventing the frustrating dropping of objects.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Volar wrist splint.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks to arrange nerve conduction studies if symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or urgent care if the fingers become permanently white/blue, completely numb to all touch, or if progressive, visible muscle wasting occurs at the base of the thumb.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; nerve conduction studies may be required for definitive confirmation.
Clinical Course: Symptoms are highly likely to improve with nocturnal splinting and ergonomic adjustments. If conservative measures fail, corticosteroid injections or surgical release are highly effective options.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the pain completely stops responding to medication or if you develop new weakness in the arm above the wrist.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The median nerve is being squeezed in a tight tunnel at the wrist, usually worsened by bending the wrist during sleep. A splint holds the wrist neutral, opening the tunnel and giving the nerve space to heal.
Actionable Steps: Obtain and wear a wrist splint tonight. Take anti-inflammatories with food. Modify workstation ergonomics tomorrow.

